      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\sqrt{-x-1}?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's re-write the expression, as follows:\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{-x-1}=\\sqrt{-(x+1)}\n$$\n\nTo obtain the graph of $y=\\sqrt{-(x+1)},$ we can perform the following transformations:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n- Reflect the graph in the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\sqrt{-x}.$\n\nThe result is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14238243/assets/d078ff010c5bb8a05bfe08bd6585ebb334bda324c69f7c8498bde38dbd8cd234.png)\n\n- Translate the result to the left by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=\\sqrt{-(x+1)}.$\n\nThe result is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14238243/assets/2fcd6900faf5246202403591c333f30a04ad6d5f51894ab509c229234976fc5b.png)",
